# Rules Rotproof keeps

{kept}

These rules come with the Rotproof version the project pins, and change only when the project upgrades it. The
project's own map, its differences and its own rules are in `AGENTS.md`.

## Running Rotproof

- **`rotproof check` checks {checked}, and fails when one breaks a rule.**
  It is the project's CI. It prints what it did not check, and why.
- **`rotproof create` makes what `.config/rotproof.toml` declares and the tree lacks, and rewrites the files Rotproof
  generates:** this file, `docs/index.md`, and `docs/knowledge/index.md` and `docs/knowledge/rules.md`. It never
  changes another file, apart from adding the fields the declaration lacks, and never moves or deletes one. Nothing
  runs it on its own.
- **`rotproof index` rewrites `docs/index.md`, `docs/knowledge/index.md` and `docs/knowledge/rules.md`** after a
  knowledge document changes. Never edit them by hand.
- **`rotproof follows knowledge/<slug>.md <key>...` pins keys in a knowledge document's `follows`** at the hash of the
  code as it is now. Run it once the document is reviewed against the code, when it
  starts to follow something and when `rotproof check` names it, rather than editing the hashes by hand.
- **Upgrade Rotproof in a commit of its own:** change the pinned version, install it, run `rotproof init`, then
  `rotproof check`. `rotproof init` applies what the new version brings to the files `rotproof create` wrote once
  (except the updates `declined` lists in `.config/rotproof.toml`), rewrites this file, and sets `files` there to the
  new version. Until it has run, `rotproof check` fails. What an update cannot do without a person, it says: do it by
  hand, or decline it by its name in `declined`. It never moves or rewrites a record. When the new version changes the
  layer table (0.4.0 built it on the onion architecture), `rotproof init` says where the code of each layer that is
  gone goes, and moves no code: move it by hand, as `rotproof check` names it until it is moved.
{layers}
## The records

Rotproof keeps two records in `docs/`: knowledge, how things are now and why, and the log, what each task left behind.
Open work is no record here: it is kept in issues. The rest of `docs/` is the project's, and Rotproof does not read it.

- **Before starting work or deploying, read `docs/knowledge/index.md`, and open only the documents that concern the
  work.** Its alarms, under `# When something happens`, are watched only by someone reading it. When something that
  used to work has stopped, search `docs/` for the message as it is first. Read `docs/log.md` when you need to know
  what went wrong before and what was learned.
- **Read a day from the clock, never from memory.** An agent has no clock of its own: a day it writes into `docs/`
  (the log's date headings, `verified`, `stale_after`) from a guess runs ahead of the clock or stays behind it.
  `rotproof check` fails on a day that has not come; a day that is behind passes unseen.
- **Keep a piece of work that can stop in the middle in an issue of its own.** Its body is the state as it is now:
  the day, how far the work came, what comes next, what has not been checked, the open questions, and links to the
  branch and the pull request. Overwrite it whenever you stop, so that whoever resumes reads one page; continue from
  it rather than starting again. Add what was measured, and what was decided and why, as dated comments, and never
  rewrite one. Before closing the issue, write what still holds in `docs/knowledge/` and what happened in the log,
  and name both in a last comment.
- **A knowledge document holds only what the code cannot tell:** why the code is so and what was rejected, how it
  should behave, and what lies outside the repository (the promises of a service it calls, the data in production,
  how to run or operate it). It is no summary of how the code works: the code says that, and a copy goes stale. When
  a person gives a reason or corrects you, or a decision of the task binds the changes after it, ask the person
  whether to keep it, and write what they answer rather than what the code suggests; do not write one for every
  change. How a document is written is in `docs/knowledge/rules.md`, which Rotproof writes; the project's own rules
  go in another guide beside it.
- **A finding is never left in a comment.** Fix it now, write it as one line in the task's issue (an issue of its
  own when it outlives the task), or let it go, and take the comment out. `rotproof check` fails on `TODO`, `FIXME`,
  `XXX`, `HACK` and `NOTE` in comments in the code. A comment says why the code beside it is written so, without the
  word; what spans several files, how to run or operate something, and what lies outside the repository go in
  `docs/knowledge/`, tied to the code by `follows`. If the declaration has `todo_link`, work that an existing issue
  already tracks may also stay as `TODO(#123): what is left` (`number`) or `TODO(ABC-123): what is left` (`key`), on
  one line, and `rotproof check` lists every such comment on each run and fails nothing for it. That list is an
  inventory and asks for nothing, so never delete a comment only to shorten it. Write only the number or key of an
  issue you created or looked up, never one made up to pass the check: `rotproof check` does not look at whether the
  issue exists. Whether a project declares `todo_link` is a person's decision: never add, remove or change it in
  `.config/rotproof.toml` to pass the check.
- **When a task is done, add one entry to `docs/log.md`, under a `## YYYY-MM-DD` heading for the day, newest first.**
  The log is a flat list of entries under the dates (OKF 0.2, section 9): one list item per task, with its fields as
  nested items, and no other headings. `rotproof check` fails on anything else. An entry keeps only what is found
  nowhere else, searchable in the repository when something breaks: what changed is in the commits, what was
  measured and decided along the way in the task's issue, and what still holds in `docs/knowledge/`.

  ```markdown
  * **<Task name>**
    * **Issue**: The task's issue (owner/repo#n), when it has one.
    * **Problems**: What went wrong, the cause, and the workaround. "None" if none.
    * **Lessons**: What to do differently next time, and where it was recorded if it became a rule.
  ```
